 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** The project runs the socket tier itself and accepts the connection-capacity and reconnect work that comes with it, in exchange for fast updates and no realtime vendor in the delivery path. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the cheapest to operate and meets the 15-second freshness goal, but it costs several seconds of lag on every change and spends repeated reads on an unchanged board. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it reduces connection operations most, but it carries recurring per-connection fees and couples the board to a provider's subscription model and availability, against the tenet preferring infrastructure the project can replace itself.
